  <section class="sec">
    <div class="kicker">Outliers explained</div>
    <h2>What sits behind each exception</h2>
    <p class="note">Every figure above that is not zero, or that differs from what the headline suggests, is accounted for here from the structured fields of the completed records concerned. No narrative text is reproduced.</p>

    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">2.6% of envenomation cases did not record the specific problem. Which five?</div><div class="a">Five completed forms where the member reached and passed the specific-problem step without selecting an option. All five received sting-typical treatment (ice or hot water); two of the five are classified major with no referral and no ambulance. The workflow allows the question to be skipped on completion; making it mandatory would close the gap.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">0.3% of injury cases and 1.8% of environmental cases did not record the specific problem.</div><div class="a">Three completed injury forms and one completed environmental form (treatment recorded as "Other") skipped the step. None is classified major.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Eleven of twelve suspected drownings are major. The twelfth?</div><div class="a">A member of the public aged 12, recorded as a non-fatal suspected drowning, conscious at discharge, treatment recorded as "Other", no referral, no ambulance called, no incident number and no narrative. The structured record describes a low-acuity episode that the crew did not escalate, which is why it was not classified major; the absence of any narrative or incident link for a suspected drowning is itself a consistency point for the action-form work.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Three of four mental health cases are major. The fourth?</div><div class="a">A panic attack in a 27-year-old, no treatment required, no referral. Classified minor consistently with those fields.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Five resuscitation-nature cases but seven resuscitation records?</div><div class="a">Two cardiac arrests were recorded under the medical nature, and the members completed the resuscitation question set for them. Counting by the question set, seven resuscitations were performed; counting by nature, five. The question-set count is the reliable one.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Envenomation is 3.6% major. Which seven cases?</div><div class="a">Three stingray injuries (two to hospital with an adult, one by ambulance), one combined medical and envenomation case transported by ambulance, one jellyfish sting with no referral, and the two majors with no specific problem and no referral described above. The stingray cases drive the severity; the last three are classification inconsistencies rather than clinical escalations.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Thirty-eight completed cases were documented a day or more after treatment. Who are they?</div><div class="a">Thirty-one are injuries and ten are major. Eighteen come from club-event cohorts, and two volunteer clubs account for sixteen of the thirty-eight. The pattern is retrospective entry after club events at a small number of units, not a system-wide lag.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Three cases show treatment after the form was created.</div><div class="a">The recorded treatment time is 44 minutes, 10 hours and 9 days later than the form's creation. These are treatment-time entry errors (the time was entered in the future relative to when the form was opened) and were excluded from the timeliness segments.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Twenty-four completed forms (1.7%) have no nature recorded.</div><div class="a">All twenty-four have a free-text reason for presentation and a treatment, and the workflow marks all steps complete. The nature question can be passed without an answer; it should be mandatory, because every downstream question set keys off it.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">232 completed cases are classified major but 253 are linked to an incident number.</div><div class="a">The two flags are set independently: 5 major cases have no incident number and 26 incident-linked cases are not classified major, 31 completed records (2.2%) where the classification and the link disagree. A further 17 open forms are flagged major with no incident link (see the open forms section). This is the consistency problem the action form is designed to remove.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Fourteen methoxyflurane records but eight pain trajectories.</div><div class="a">Six of the fourteen have no starting pain score entered. Two of the eight scored cases start at 1 of 10; the remaining six start at 8 to 10 and fall by 0 to 6 points within four minutes.</div></div>
  </section>

