  <section class="sec">
    <div class="kicker">Summary</div>
    <h2>Statistics for presentation</h2>
    <ol class="list">
      <li>Cutover in three days: last old form 9 November, first new form 12 November; 1,494 new-form cases lodged in season one with no parallel running, 1,420 of them completed.</li>
      <li>Median 4 minutes from opening a form to its final edit; 95.0 per cent of lodged forms completed.</li>
      <li>87.0 per cent of completed cases documented within one hour of treatment, measurable for the first time this season.</li>
      <li>On completed forms, at most 2.6 per cent of cases in any clinical branch did not record the specific problem, with every gap accounted for.</li>
      <li>Unidentified patients more than halved, from 27.9 to 12.5 per cent.</li>
      <li>Seven resuscitations with method, rhythm, shocks, cycles and handover state recorded on every one; the old form carried any detail on half of comparable cases.</li>
      <li>Oxygen (47) and methoxyflurane (14) administrations with structured audit records; recorded pain falling from a mean of 7.1 by 2.6 points within four minutes.</li>
      <li>984 injuries, 195 envenomations, 137 medical and 57 environmental presentations, each with specific problems, body locations, treatments and discharge pathways counted directly.</li>
      <li>Seven peer-support requests surfaced through a question that previously did not exist.</li>
      <li>Form improvement is now empirical: free-text overflow rates identify the exact option lists to fix.</li>
      <li>126 per cent six-season growth in presentations; January carries 43.3 per cent of the season and weekends 51.3 per cent.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="kicker">Where to next</div>
    <h2>Two programs that build on this work</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>One action form</h3>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px">
        <p>The first aid form and the rescue form will be combined into a single action form with one clear definition for members: one form for one person assisted. This removes the need for a member to remember to complete one or both forms, to decide which form applies, or to enter the same personal details twice. It will be one form, with questions presented dynamically for the rescue, for the first aid, or for both.</p>
        <p>The result is a record that is accurately captured, reported cohesively, and linked to incidents consistently. The 31 completed records in this season's data where the major classification and the incident link disagree, the 17 open forms flagged major with no link, and the suspected drowning with no incident number, are the kind of inconsistency the single form is designed to remove at source. Above all, the organisation gains consistency and completeness of data that allow it to be more informed than at any previous point.</p>
        <p>The first aid form project has been a key signifier of what is possible when the system meets the need, and of the benefits of accurate, complete and consistent data.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Incident categorisation</h3>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px">
        <p>Following this work, a similar program will be applied to the incident categorisation approach. It will simplify categorisation for members, capture more accurately what is being done on the beach, and make the system easier to use and understand, with clear standards on how information is captured.</p>
        <p>Stronger data quality and consistency will in turn support operational decisions, reporting, funding submissions and everything else that good data enables.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="kicker">Limitations</div>
    <h2>What this data cannot support</h2>
    <ul class="limits">
      <li>The workbook contains no user-satisfaction survey; satisfaction evidence is behavioural (completion rate, speed, adoption, answer rates).</li>
      <li>Resuscitation (7) and methoxyflurane (14) counts are small and are reported as counts, not precise rates.</li>
      <li>Old and new taxonomies differ; cross-form trend comparisons use shared categories only.</li>
      <li>Timestamps measure documentation behaviour, not care delivery.</li>
      <li>Season-on-season volume comparisons use a matched November-to-June window because the 2024/25 export spans all months, and season totals include open forms.</li>
      <li>Unit type is derived from unit names in the data (volunteers, lifeguards, aquatic sports, support), not from a separate classification.</li>
      <li>The old-form comparison figures (2024/25) include every lodged case because the old export has no open state; the 2025/26 figures use completed cases only.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

